[section:von_mises_dist Von Mises Distribution]

``#include <boost/math/distributions/von_mises.hpp>``

   namespace boost{ namespace math{
    template <typename RealType = double,
              typename ``__Policy``   = ``__policy_class`` >
    class von_mises_distribution;

    using von_mises = von_mises_distribution<>;

    template <typename RealType, typename ``__Policy``>
    class von_mises_distribution
    {
    public:
       using value_type = RealType;
       using policy_type = Policy;

       explicit von_mises_distribution(RealType mean = 0, RealType concentration = 1);

       RealType mean() const;
       RealType concentration() const;
       RealType location() const;
       RealType scale() const;
    };

   }} // namespaces

The [@https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Von_Mises_distribution von Mises distribution],
also known as the circular normal distribution, is a continuous probability distribution of an angle.
With mean [mu] and concentration [kappa] its probability density function is

[expression f(x; [mu], [kappa]) = e[super [kappa] cos(x - [mu])] / (2[pi] I[sub 0]([kappa]))]

for [mu] - [pi] <= x <= [mu] + [pi], where I[sub 0] is the modified Bessel function of the first kind.
With [kappa] = 0 it is the uniform distribution on the circle,
and as [kappa] grows it approaches a normal distribution with variance 1/[kappa].

[h4 Member Functions]

   explicit von_mises_distribution(RealType mean = 0, RealType concentration = 1);

Constructs a von Mises distribution with mean /mean/ and concentration /concentration/.

Requires that /mean/ is finite and that /concentration/ is finite and non-negative;
otherwise calls __domain_error.

   RealType mean() const;
   RealType location() const;

Return the /mean/ parameter of this distribution.

   RealType concentration() const;
   RealType scale() const;

Return the /concentration/ parameter of this distribution.

[h4 Non-member Accessors]

All the [link math_toolkit.dist_ref.nmp usual non-member accessor functions]
that are generic to all distributions are supported: __usual_accessors.

The support is one turn centred on the mean, [mu] - [pi] <= x <= [mu] + [pi].
Outside it the pdf is zero and the cdf is 0 or 1.

The mean, median and mode are all [mu], and the skewness is zero.
The moments are those of circular statistics:
`variance` returns the circular variance 1 - I[sub 1]([kappa])/I[sub 0]([kappa]),
and `standard_deviation` returns the circular standard deviation
[sqrt](-2 ln(I[sub 1]([kappa])/I[sub 0]([kappa]))),
so the standard deviation is not the square root of the variance.

[h4 Accuracy]

The pdf and cdf have full relative accuracy in both tails, at any precision,
apart from the growth of error inherent in exp([kappa] cos(x - [mu])) when [kappa] is large.
The plots below compare `double` against `cpp_bin_float_50`,
with the condition number of the function drawn as an envelope;
they are generated by reporting/accuracy/von_mises_ulps.cpp.

*PDF with concentration of 4*

[$../images/von_mises_ulps_pdf_4d.svg]

*PDF with concentration of 64*

[$../images/von_mises_ulps_pdf_64d.svg]

*CDF with concentration of 4*

[$../images/von_mises_ulps_cdf_4d.svg]

*CDF with concentration of 64*

[$../images/von_mises_ulps_cdf_64d.svg]

[h4 Implementation]

The normalization e[super -[kappa]]I[sub 0]([kappa]) is computed from __cyl_bessel_i,
or from its asymptotic series when e[super [kappa]] would overflow.

For x <= [mu] the cdf is

[expression F(x) = e[super -2[kappa] sin[super 2](a/2)] / (2[pi] e[super -[kappa]]I[sub 0]([kappa])) [int][sub 0][super [pi]-a] e[super -2[kappa] sin(a + s/2) sin(s/2)] ds]

with a = [mu] - x. The integrand is positive, so there is no cancellation in the left tail,
and it decays like e[super -[kappa] s sin(a)], so the range is cut where it falls below machine epsilon.
Up to double precision the integral is evaluated with a 30-point Gauss-Legendre rule, and beyond that with
[link math_toolkit.double_exponential.de_tanh_sinh tanh-sinh] quadrature. For x > [mu] the cdf is 1 - F(2[mu] - x), and the complement follows by symmetry.

The circular variance is computed as e[super -[kappa]](I[sub 0]([kappa]) - I[sub 1]([kappa])) / e[super -[kappa]]I[sub 0]([kappa]),
never as a difference of the Bessel functions:
for moderate [kappa] both are integrals of positive functions over a full period, which the trapezoidal rule evaluates to geometric accuracy,
and for large [kappa] every term of their asymptotic series is positive.

The quantile is found by Newton iteration on the cdf, starting from the normal approximation.

[h4 References]

* [@https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Von_Mises_distribution Wikipedia von Mises distribution]
* [@https://mathworld.wolfram.com/vonMisesDistribution.html Weisstein, Eric W. "von Mises Distribution." From MathWorld--A Wolfram Web Resource.]
* K. V. Mardia and P. E. Jupp, ['Directional Statistics], Wiley, 2000.

